{"kind":"report","text":"ready-for-review at exact clean head 1c872077bf7d034b93e048268cc1f0214c76738d, artifact 816396808524f5fc72619ea9573e157f81f5a0ba. Each finding below says what closed it and how it was demonstrated rather than asserted.\n\nF1, temporaries. The status dump went to /tmp/gs-status.json and the generated retirement acts to retire-dot.json at the worktree root. Both now go under .tmp, which the step creates with mkdir -p, and the batch reads from there. Demonstrated by the absence of residue: `grep -n '/tmp/\\|\u003e retire-dot' AGENTS.md` returns nothing, and the worktree is clean at the reported head with no untracked file.\n\nF2, the quiet-wave check, and this is the one worth reading. The old form required a request's artifact reference to resolve through the live-artifact map before counting it, so a review request naming an unmerged head or carrying a literal unexpanded placeholder was dropped from the total in silence. The fix is to separate two questions the old form conflated: what makes a request a review request is that it names an artifact at all, not that the name resolves. So every live request naming an artifact is counted unless that artifact already has a verdict, and a second figure, `unresolved`, reports how many names did not resolve.\n\nDemonstrated against the live log rather than a fixture, as the conditions ask. Running both forms over the current projection: the old form reports 8 awaiting, the new form reports 13, and 36 named references do not resolve through the live-artifact map. So the gate would have declared quiet with at least five review requests outstanding. The gap between 36 unresolved and 5 extra awaiting is not a discrepancy — most unresolvable references belong to requests whose artifact already carries a verdict, so they are settled either way.\n\nF3, artifact liveness. Every step that derived artifacts by filtering statements on kind name now reads projection.artifacts, joining statements only for fields artifacts lack. The projection's artifact objects carry path and commit directly, so the selectors lost their .body prefix as part of the change. Demonstrated by admitting a refused record: injecting one statement with kind artifact that the fold never ruled effective moves the old statements-filter count from 55 to 56 and leaves the projection.artifacts count at 55. The request was right that today's agreement was luck; it is now construction.\n\nOn the condition that the head merges cleanly with current main: it did not, and I recorded that separately at 16eea7fa. Main restructured the documentation after the branch point, so docs/reference.md no longer exists and docs/getting-started.md was split into docs/how-to and docs/reference pages. Four conflicts, resolved deliberately at 2bd4dc46. The one that mattered was AGENTS.md step 5, changed on both sides: the branch added the per-path retire-and-publish rule and main gained the self-initiated-work exception I merged earlier today. Both survive; taking either side alone would have silently reverted the other. `git merge-tree` now reports this head merging clean with main.\n\nOne thing left open and deliberately not smuggled in here. The branch's operative path-choosing guidance — the four cases, the bare-supersede authority rule, the warning about path dot — lived in the two files main re-homed, and it is not in the tree now. AGENTS.md keeps the rule and the four cases, so the contract is intact, but the how-to and reference pages that walked a reader through it need rewriting into the new structure. That is follow-up, and it should be its own request rather than a rider on this one.\n\nGates: `go vet ./...` clean, `go test ./internal/docset -count=1` passes, worktree clean at the reported head.","body":{"head":"1c872077bf7d034b93e048268cc1f0214c76738d","status":"ready-for-review"}}